 119:Core/Src/hal.h **** /*
 120:Core/Src/hal.h **** 
 121:Core/Src/hal.h ****     - Making a BSRR pin 1 will mean a write or reset happens to the pin number
 122:Core/Src/hal.h ****     on ODR which stores the output and a 0 means ODR will not be assigned
 123:Core/Src/hal.h ****     - if you pass in 0, to write 0 you need to reset so you need to make 1 the bits of 16 to 31
 124:Core/Src/hal.h ****     on BSRR which will reset the bit on ODR
ARM GAS  /tmp/cciTSmtz.s 			page 10


 125:Core/Src/hal.h ****     - There are only 0 to 15 bits on ODR
 126:Core/Src/hal.h ****     - You can modify the ODR directly but writing one bit require a read(taking ODR value 
 127:Core/Src/hal.h ****     into register as you want to preserve the values in other bits), modify(bitwise 
 128:Core/Src/hal.h ****     OR), write(back to ODR) series of steps
 129:Core/Src/hal.h ****     - This violates atomicity so after you have read if there is an interrupt and 
 130:Core/Src/hal.h ****     it modifies some other bit of same ODR and then your modify and write happens
 131:Core/Src/hal.h ****     , this would mean that the modification made by interrupt would be lost as you
 132:Core/Src/hal.h ****     modify old read and then write that back
 133:Core/Src/hal.h ****     - With BSRR you directly write(look at the '=') to the register and there is 
 134:Core/Src/hal.h ****     no read or modify loop when you change data of BSRR in the software and in same 
 135:Core/Src/hal.h ****     cycle of writing to BSRR the ODR value is updated as well
 136:Core/Src/hal.h ****     - The whole bit (re)/setting of ODR is done in one cycle so atomic
 137:Core/Src/hal.h ****     */
